MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

To remove doublecrossover events with only single markers, Karl wrote the function cleanGeno(). It has a limitation to be only working for maps based on 2 genotypes, A or B.

I just tested it on a F2 population (A, H, B) and it seems to work fine as well at least using maxmark = 1.

Could you tell me what could go wrong applying your function to 3 genotype data?

Thank you,
Michel

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Wed Dec 16 13:59:55 2015
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Wed, 16 Dec 2015 13:59:55 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] cleanGeno for maps with 3 genotypes
Message-ID: <23HhLVRzT-E.KAgbmzePBgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <23HhLVRzT-E.bU2BLYeBBgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <23HhLVRzT-E.bU2BLYeBBgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/23HhLVRzT-E/m/KAgbmzePBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The cleanGeno() function looks for tight double crossovers, but in an intercross, these can be real, because there could be one on each chromosome; for example AB -> AA -> AB might be AAB|BAA.

karl

